</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"> Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities </h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">JinLi He
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Liang Bai
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xian Yang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>
J. He and L. Bai are with Institute of Intelligent Information Processing, Shanxi University, Taiyuan, 030006, China (Corresponding author: Liang Bai)
<br class="ltx_break">Email: hejinli@sxu.edu.cn, bailiang@sxu.edu.cn
X. Yang is with Alliance Manchester Business School, The University of Manchester, Manchester, M13 9PL, UK. Email: xian.yang@manchester.ac.uk </span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Rehearsal is one of the key techniques for mitigating catastrophic forgetting and has been widely adopted in continual learning algorithms due to its simplicity and practicality. However, the theoretical understanding of how rehearsal scale influences learning dynamics remains limited. To address this gap, we formulate rehearsal-based continual learning as a multidimensional effectiveness–driven iterative optimization problem, providing a unified characterization across diverse performance metrics. Within this framework, we derive a closed-form analysis of adaptability, memorability, and generalization from the perspective of rehearsal scale. Our results uncover several intriguing and counterintuitive findings. First, rehearsal can impair model’s adaptability, in sharp contrast to its traditionally recognized benefits. Second, increasing the rehearsal scale does not necessarily improve memory retention. When tasks are similar and noise levels are low, the memory error exhibits a diminishing lower bound. Finally, we validate these insights through numerical simulations and extended analyses on deep neural networks across multiple real-world datasets, revealing statistical patterns of rehearsal mechanisms in continual learning.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Continual Learning, Learning Theory, Incremental Learning.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intelligent systems need to acquire, update, and accumulate knowledge throughout their lifecycle to adapt to the dynamically changing real world, a capability known as continual learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Typically, continual learning machines are challenged by catastrophic forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, where performance on previous tasks degrades dramatically due to parameter updates when learning new tasks. As new knowledge replaces previous knowledge, the model’s adaptation performance improves while memorability diminishes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Earlier efforts have attempted to address this problem by preserving previously learned knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. However, recent work has focused more on facilitating the adaptability of new knowledge and the generalizability of models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. These efforts have deepened the understanding of continual learning: an ideal continual learning learner should strike an effective and delicate balance between retaining previously learned knowledge and acquiring new knowledge, while also being sufficiently generalizable to accommodate differences in unseen data distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In biological systems, hippocampal replay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> has been proposed as a system-level mechanism that consolidates memories and improves the generalization by reactivating previously experienced scenes. Although biological and artificial systems differ significantly, they exhibit intriguing parallels: both consolidate knowledge and accelerate learning from past experiences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Similar to biological systems, rehearsal mechanism performs better in continual learning to resist catastrophic forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Despite advances in the empirical performance of rehearsal-based methods, the theoretical understanding of how the rehearsal mechanism impacts continual learning, even in simple models, is not yet fully understood: When does a continual learning model benefit from rehearsal? Will the rehearsal scale itself adversely impact performance?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20791v1/lianlian6.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/144;" width="476" height="144" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An ideal continual learning system should strike a delicate balance among the adaptation of newly acquired knowledge, the memorization of previously learned knowledge, and the generalization of unseen data distributions across diverse scenarios.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the aforementioned questions by theoretically elucidating the limitations of rehearsal scale. We establish a theoretical characterization of rehearsal-based continual learning from three complementary perspectives, highlighting both its strengths and inherent drawbacks. The main contributions are summarized as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive closed-form expressions characterizing theoretical error along three evaluation dimensions, revealing how rehearsal scale influences performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that increasing the rehearsal scale does not necessarily lead to better performance: rehearsal can hinder adaptability, and there is a decreasing floor in memory error under the overfitting regimes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate these theoretical findings through numerical simulations and futher extend them with experiments using DNNs on multiple real-world datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research in continual learning has primarily focused on empirical studies. These methods can be broadly categorized into three main categories: rehearsal-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, where a portion of the previous task data is stored and replayed to mitigate forgetting while learning new tasks. Expansion-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> allocate separate network parameters to learn tasks without interfering with previously learned ones, and recent research leverages pre-trained models with lightweight adaptations such as prompting or adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Regularization-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> constrain the parameters crucial to previous tasks when learning new tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical studies in continual learning mainly focus on elucidating its dynamic evolutionary mechanisms through diverse frameworks and establishing links to related domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> reformulates continual learning as a combination of within-task prediction and task-id prediction, with links to out-of-distribution detection. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> proposes a general formulation of ideal continual learning, linking it to related areas and providing generalization bounds for replayed samples. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> focuses on regularization-based continual learning estimators and demonstrates the phenomenon of benign overfitting in continual learning. Connections between continual learning and alternating projections or Kaczmarz methods have also been explored, leading to worst-case forgetting bounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. The trade-off between forgetting and generalization is modeled as a two-player game solved via dynamic programming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> studies continual ridge regression with non-random features, focusing on the impact of regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have investigated rehearsal-based continual learning from multiple perspectives, examining the effects of rehearsal strategies<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, sampling schemes<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, step sizes<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, and network width<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> analyzed the role of model dimensionality, highlighting the potential benefits of increased model dimensionality for both multi-task and continual learning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> compared concurrent and sequential rehearsal strategies, showing that sequential rehearsal outperforms concurrent rehearsal when tasks differ substantially. In contrast to prior work, we focus on the impact of rehearsal scale, a critical yet underexplored factor, and uncover a series of intriguing and counterintuitive findings. Specifically, we show that increasing rehearsal scale can impair model adaptability, a conclusion that contrasts sharply with the conventional view. We validate these theoretical insights through numerical simulations and extensive experiments with deep neural networks on multiple real-world datasets, revealing the double-edged effect of rehearsal. These theoretical findings provide new perspectives into the role of rehearsal scale in continual learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data.</span>  
We consider a standard continual learning problem where tasks are introduced sequentially, indexed by <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,...,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,...,T</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that each task <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> holds a dataset <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}=\left\{(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})\in\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}\right\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}=\left\{(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})\in\mathbb{R}^{p}\times\mathbb{R}\right\}_{i=1}^{n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its sample size. Here, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the feature vector and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the corresponding response variable. Each pair <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t,i},y_{t,i})</annotation></semantics></math> follows model <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}+\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}+\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is random noise and <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the optimal parameter of the <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th task specific model. The equation above can be rewritten into a compact matrix equation for training samples:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}_{t}=\boldsymbol{X}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}_{t}=\boldsymbol{X}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the matrix <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}_{t}:=[x_{t,1},x_{t,2},...,x_{t,n_{t}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}_{t}:=[x_{t,1},x_{t,2},...,x_{t,n_{t}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}_{t}:=[y_{t,1},y_{t,2},...,y_{t,n_{t}}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}_{t}:=[y_{t,1},y_{t,2},...,y_{t,n_{t}}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}:=[\epsilon_{t,1},\epsilon_{t,2},...,\epsilon_{t,n_{t}}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}:=[\epsilon_{t,1},\epsilon_{t,2},...,\epsilon_{t,n_{t}}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For analytical tractability, the Gaussian features and noise are adopted in this setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption III.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, element of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows i.i.d standard Gaussian. The noise <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is independently drawn from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(0,\sigma_{t}^{2}I_{n_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(0,\sigma_{t}^{2}I_{n_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> denotes noise level.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark III.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Gaussian setup is used to exploit the properties of orthogonal projection inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption III.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The samples <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}=n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark III.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that analyzing this model provides a critical first step toward understanding deep neural networks, as shown in recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, and our results are further validated through DNNs on real-world datasets with longer task sequences and deeper architectures in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Empirical Validation on DNNs ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Avsp_orth_1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S3.F2.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(a)</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Avss_same_1.png" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S3.F2.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(b)</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Avsp_sim_sigma002_1.png" id="S3.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(c)</p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Adaptation error of rehearsal-based methods under different setups,
where <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\|^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\|^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subfigure settings: (a) <math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=500</annotation></semantics></math>; (b) <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>; (c) <math id="S3.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=500</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>. Discrete points denote averages across runs. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation metrics.</span>  
For estimator <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote its estimation error by <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}})=\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}}-\boldsymbol{w}^{*}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}})=\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}}-\boldsymbol{w}^{*}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the adaptation error (<math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, generalization error (<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and memory error (<math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> can be defined as follows</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{A}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\right\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{A}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{G}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{t}\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{G}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{t}\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{M}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\frac{1}{t-1}\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}[\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}-\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{i}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{M}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right):=\frac{1}{t-1}\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}[\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}-\left\|\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{i}}-\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the parameters after task t has been learned. Due to the parameter differences reflect functional differences, the distance between optimal parameters is defined to measure inter-task similarity. A algorithm obtains increasing performance on previous tasks if, for each <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, the forgetting error satisfies <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{w}_{t}}\right)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rehearsal-based Continual Learning Estimator.</span> The rehearsal-based continual estimator assumes tasks arrive sequentially and preserves knowledge of previous tasks by storing a subset of their samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. For each task <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=2,...,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=2,...,T</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that a total of <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> samples are stored. Specifically, we assume that the feature vector matrix of the <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th previous task ( <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,...,t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,...,t-1</annotation></semantics></math> ) stored in the memory buffer is <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times\frac{s}{t-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times\frac{s}{t-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the corresponding response variable denoted as <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{g}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{s}{t-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{g}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{s}{t-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The training process converges to the optimal solution by minimizing the training loss, formulated as the following optimization problem: <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t}^{(\text{Reh})}:=\underset{\boldsymbol{w}}{\arg\min}\left\|\boldsymbol{X}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{y}_{t}\right\|^{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\left\|\boldsymbol{Z}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{g}_{i}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Reh</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝒘</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{t}^{(\text{Reh})}:=\underset{\boldsymbol{w}}{\arg\min}\left\|\boldsymbol{X}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{y}_{t}\right\|^{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\left\|\boldsymbol{Z}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{g}_{i}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;n+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;n+s</annotation></semantics></math> (overparameterized), multiple solutions exist that achieve zero training loss. In this case, we select the solution with the minimum <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm , i.e., the optimization problem:
<math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{w}}\bigl\{\|\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{w}_{t-1}\|^{2},\text{ s.t. }(\boldsymbol{X}_{t})^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}=\boldsymbol{y}_{t},(\boldsymbol{Z}_{i})^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}=\boldsymbol{g}_{i},i=1,\dots,t-1\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>𝒘</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\min_{\boldsymbol{w}}\bigl\{\|\boldsymbol{w}-\boldsymbol{w}_{t-1}\|^{2},\text{ s.t. }(\boldsymbol{X}_{t})^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}=\boldsymbol{y}_{t},(\boldsymbol{Z}_{i})^{\top}\boldsymbol{w}=\boldsymbol{g}_{i},i=1,\dots,t-1\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Among all overfitting solutions, we focus on the minimum <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm solution because it corresponds to the convergence point of stochastic gradient descent in continual learning. In Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we provide theoretical results for rehearsal-based learner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The impact of rehearsal scale on adaptability, memorability, and generalization.</span> Unlike prior studies that focused on other foundational factors, we theoretically analyze how rehearsal scale affects performance. The adaptation error measures fit to the current task, the memory error captures performance loss on previous tasks, and the generalization error evaluates transfer to unseen tasks. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> explores the relationship between rehearsal scale and these three aspects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Main Results</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the main results. For rehearsal-based continual learning, we establish three theorems that characterize adaptation, memory and generalization error under overparameterized and underparameterized regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Adaptation error)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption III.1. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a>-<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption III.3. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></a> hold. Then the adaptation error of the rehearsal-based continual learning is formally given by: 
<br class="ltx_break">under the overparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}\right\|^{2}+a_{\text{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">noise</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}\right\|^{2}+a_{\text{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^{T}\lambda^{T-k}\frac{n+s}{p}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term A1}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Term A1</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^{T}\lambda^{T-k}\frac{n+s}{p}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term A1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">under the underparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})\right]=\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})\right]=\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:=\frac{p-n-s}{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:=\frac{p-n-s}{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{noise}:=\frac{(1-\lambda^{T})p\sigma^{2}}{(p-n-s-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{noise}:=\frac{(1-\lambda^{T})p\sigma^{2}}{(p-n-s-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, with larger <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> indicating greater overparameterization.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is provided in Appendix B. It describes the ability to fit current task, forming the basis for analyzing how it learns new knowledge and retains previous knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increasing the rehearsal size enhances the model’s adaptability under underparameterization, whereas it can be detrimental under overparameterization.</span> Specifically, when <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+s&gt;p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+s&gt;p+1</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation (6), <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> increases, indicating that more playback contributes to better adaptation. When slightly overparameterized in Equation (5), we have <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\approx n+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\approx n+s</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>. At this point, Term A1 and the denominator in Term <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> approach zero when tasks are similar, and thus <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{noise}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{noise}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates and causes <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> to be increasing w.r.t. <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. When heavily overparameterized in Equation (5), Term A1 is close to zero, and thus <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{A}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> increases when <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is low. Intuitively, when tasks are similar, the model can leverage rehearsal more effectively, leading to improvements in performance on current task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Mvsp_same.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F3.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(a)</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Mvss_same.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F3.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(b)</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2602.20791v1/muni/Mvsp_sim_sigma002.png" id="S4.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:153/89;" width="153" height="89" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(c)</p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Memory error of rehearsal-based methods under different setups,
where <math id="S4.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\|^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{w}_{t}^{*}\|^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subfigure settings: (a) <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=500</annotation></semantics></math>; (b) <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>; (c) <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=500</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>. Discrete points denote averages over simulation runs. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The impact of rehearsal size was also verified through numerical simulations in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b), where average adaptation error is plotted against rehearsal size for different model parameters. The red curve marked with ”<math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>”, decreases in the underparameterized regime (<math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>) but first decreases and then increases in the overparameterized regime (<math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>), which validates our earlier insights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark IV.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Turning point.)
Based on the above analysis, we characterize the inflection point under overparameterization by computing the partial derivative with respect to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=3000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, the inflection point of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated to be near <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the red curve shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond analyzing the impact of rehearsal size, we also examined the effects of inter-task similarity and model parameters. We found that under overparameterization, models require higher inter-task similarity to better adapt to the current task, whereas this effect is not observed in underparameterized settings (Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c)). Moreover, the overparameterization helps mitigate the impact of task variability and noise effects on model’s adaptability, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a) and Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c). Due to space constraints, a more detailed discussion of these factors is provided in Appendix G.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Memory error)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption III.1. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a> and Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption III.3. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></a>, the memory error of the rehearsal-based continual learning model is formally given by</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">under the overparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{k=1}^{T-1}\sum_{j&gt;k}^{T}\frac{n+s}{p}u_{kj}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{j}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term M1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Term M1</mtext></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{k=1}^{T-1}\sum_{j&gt;k}^{T}\frac{n+s}{p}u_{kj}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{j}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term M1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{T-1}\left(\lambda^{T}-\lambda^{i}\right)\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term M2}}+m_{\text{noise}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Term M2</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">noise</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{T-1}\left(\lambda^{T}-\lambda^{i}\right)\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{i}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term M2}}+m_{\text{noise}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">under the underparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{k=1}^{T-1}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{k=1}^{T-1}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{kj}:=\lambda^{T-k}-\lambda^{j-k}+\lambda^{T-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{kj}:=\lambda^{T-k}-\lambda^{j-k}+\lambda^{T-j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{noise}:=\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{T-1}\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{p-n-s-1}\left(\lambda^{i}-\lambda^{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{noise}:=\frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{i=1}^{T-1}\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{p-n-s-1}\left(\lambda^{i}-\lambda^{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the above Equation (7) can be reformulated as follows</span></p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(T=2)]=-\frac{(n+s)(p-n-s)}{p^{2}}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(T=2)]=-\frac{(n+s)(p-n-s)}{p^{2}}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{n+s}{p}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{(n+s)(p-n-s)\sigma^{2}}{(p-n-s-1)p}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{n+s}{p}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{(n+s)(p-n-s)\sigma^{2}}{(p-n-s-1)p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similarly, the Equation (8) can be reformulated as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(T=2)]=\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(T=2)]=\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed proof is provided in Appendix C. Based on Theorems <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem IV.3 (Memory error). ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV.3</span></a>, we further explore their analytical insights and examine the influence of factors such as rehearsal size, as well as the performance differences observed under both overparameterized and underparameterized regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increasing the rehearsal does not always lead to better memorability in continual learning models under the overparameterized regime.</span> Specifically, we consider the case where <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For the overparameterized regime result in Equation (9), when tasks are similar and <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is low, the second term dominates and causes <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> first decreases and then increases as <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> increases, indicating the existence of decreasing floor. For the underparameterized regime result in Equation (10), rehearsal no longer contributes to memorability. In this situation, the <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> depends solely on the inherent similarity between tasks ( i.e. <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> ), meaning that the memory error is fully determined by similarity between the final task and preceding ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The yellow curve marked ”<span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"> Y </span>” in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) illustrates how the average memory error varies with rehearsal size when <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=3\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=3\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the overparameterized regime (<math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>), the memory error first decreases and then increases, indicating the existence of a decreasing performance floor. In contrast, in the underparameterized regime (<math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>), the error remains unaffected, and zero forgetting is achieved when the task-optimal parameters remain consistent.</p>
</div>
