<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalization error)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption III.1. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a>-<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption III.3. ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.3</span></a>, the expected generalization error of the rehearsal-based continual learning model is formally given as follows</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">under the overparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{T}\frac{n+s}{p}\lambda^{T-k}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{j}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term G1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Term G1</mtext></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{T}\frac{n+s}{p}\lambda^{T-k}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{j}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term G1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\lambda^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term G2}}+g_{\text{noise}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Term G2</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">noise</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\lambda^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}}_{\text{Term G2}}+g_{\text{noise}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">under the underparameterized regime, there have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{T}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{k}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{noise}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T}):=\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{p-n-s-1}\left(1-\lambda^{T}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{noise}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T}):=\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{p-n-s-1}\left(1-\lambda^{T}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we reformulate Equation (11) to provide a clearer interpretation of the error form , resulting in</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(T=2)]=\frac{1}{2}(1-\lambda^{2})\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(T=2)]=\frac{1}{2}(1-\lambda^{2})\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2}\lambda^{2}(\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}\right\|^{2})+\frac{p\sigma^{2}(1-\lambda^{2})}{p-n-s-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{1}{2}\lambda^{2}(\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}\right\|^{2})+\frac{p\sigma^{2}(1-\lambda^{2})}{p-n-s-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similarly, the Equation (12) can be reformulated as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(T=2)]=\frac{1}{2}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(T=2)]=\frac{1}{2}\left\|\boldsymbol{w}_{2}^{*}-\boldsymbol{w}_{1}^{*}\right\|^{2}+\frac{p\sigma^{2}}{n+s-p-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increasing rehearsal can degrade generalization under overparameterization, especially when tasks are dissimilar.</span> Consider the case where <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>. When slightly overparameterized in Equation (13), the second term approaches zero, the denominator <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p-n-s-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p-n-s-1</annotation></semantics></math> in the third term approaches zero, and thus this term dominates. In this situation, increasing rehearsal size raises the peak generalization error. In contrast, when heavily overparameterized in Equation (13), the second term dominates and decreases as <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> increases when tasks are similar and <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is low. Moreover, the <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{G}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{w}}_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> increases in Equation (14), indicating that larger rehearsal consistently enhances generalization under underparameterization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b), average generalization error varies with rehearsal size, with optimal parameters being orthogonal. The yellow curve with markers “<span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"> Y </span>” decreases when underparameterized (<math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>), but increases with rehearsal size when overparameterized (<math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;p-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;p-n</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming these insights. Additionally, we examined other factors under different parameterization regimes. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c), increasing similarity enhances generalization in both underparameterized and overparameterized settings. However, enlarging the parameter size reduces the influence of rehearsal and inter-task similarity on generalization (Figures <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a), <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Noted that the logarithmic axes are used to capture variations in error under different parameterization regimes in Figures <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ IV Main Results ‣ Understanding the Role of Rehearsal Scale in Continual Learning under Varying Model Capacities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The horizontal axis illustrates the transition from underparameterization to overparameterization, allowing an intuitive comparison. It is worth noting that logarithmic scaling provides a wider coordinate range while preserving the original trends. Similar configurations have been used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> to reveal subtle variations in the average error.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20791v1/A_tinyimagenet_class.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/283;" width="476" height="283"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Adaptation error on Tiny-ImageNet with increasing training classes, with the legend showing varying buffer sizes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, we theoretically characterize the effects of rehearsal size on the three types of errors. The following proposition reveals the connection among adaptability, memorability and generalizability for <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and presents conditions for effective generalization performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
